'''underwrite''' (un-dėr-rīt′), ''v.''; pret. ''underwrote'', pp. ''underwritten'' (''underwrit'', pret. and pp., obsolete), ppr. ''underwriting''. [< ME. ''underwriten'', < AS. ''underwrītan'', write under, subscribe, < ''under'', under, + ''wrītan'', write.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To write below or under; subscribe.
{{smaller block|<poem>I was markid withoute mercy, and myn name entrid
In the legende of lif longe er I were;
Or ellis ''vndir-writen'' for wykkid, as witnessith the gospel.</poem>
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (A), xi. 255.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>We’ll have thee, as our rarer monsters are,
Painted upon a pole, and ''underwrit''
“Here may you see the tyrant.”</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, v. 8. 26.}}}}
'''2.''' To agree to pay by signing one’s name; subscribe.
{{smaller block|The subscription money did not come in with the same readiness with which it had been ''underwritten''.
{{right|''Beverley'', Virginia, I. ¶ 139.}}}}
Specifically—'''3.''' To agree or undertake by setting one’s name to (a policy of insurance) to become answerable for certain losses specified therein: used chiefly in marine insurance. Hence ''underwriter''.—'''4.''' To submit to; put up with. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>''Underwrite'' in an observing kind</poem>}}

